1
00:00:02,020 --> 00:00:09,220
We built KeyTurn for a gap we observed in IBM Bob's database password-change workflow.

2
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:15,580
The files were updated, but the live database still used the old password.

3
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:23,740
That leaves access open and can break the next software update, even when the application looks healthy.

4
00:00:25,060 --> 00:00:33,240
We saw this in three short-request trials on Mastodon, a real social application in our lab.

5
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:38,780
Bob edited the files and listed live changes as next steps.

6
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:41,480
It did not change the live password.

7
00:00:42,400 --> 00:00:49,640
Our checks found the old password accepted, the new password refused, and the next-update test failing.

8
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:57,240
In one run where we stated the outcome, Bob completed the change.

9
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:03,860
KeyTurn turns those requirements into repeatable checks of the running system.

10
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:12,660
It checks that the app uses the new password, old access is retired, and queued work finishes.

11
00:01:14,160 --> 00:01:17,660
It checks risky operations and saves the evidence.

12
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:23,400
Bob used KeyTurn to complete one handover on the first version.

13
00:01:24,420 --> 00:01:30,640
With the current version, it inspected an unfinished change and called it incomplete.

14
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:35,020
Now watch the current product, operated by a script.

15
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:39,460
The files are updated, but the live password is unchanged.

16
00:01:39,460 --> 00:01:47,660
KeyTurn reports FAILED and refuses a restart because the database rejects the new password.

17
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:59,160
We queue a post, start monitoring, then change the database password, reload the connection pool, and restart the apps.

18
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:05,220
Three of ten customer samples fail during the change.

19
00:02:05,220 --> 00:02:08,680
KeyTurn records that interruption.

20
00:02:10,320 --> 00:02:15,640
Then it tests fresh connections, restarts, and the queued post.

21
00:02:16,660 --> 00:02:20,500
The new password works. The old password is rejected.

22
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:25,300
Old connections are closed. The post is published.

23
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:31,780
KeyTurn reports VERIFIED and saves the handover record.

24
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:36,780
The change is complete. Tracked changes require fresh checks.

25
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:45,720
Eighty-one unit tests passed, plus checks for both supported password-change methods.

26
00:02:48,940 --> 00:02:56,100
KeyTurn. New password working. Old password rejected. A record you can check.
